Support the WCH CH390 with the Davicom DM9051 driver, and fix the driver
defects the CH390 exposed.

This is the cumulative diff of the upstream-bound commits on the fork
ptr727/zephyrproject-rtos-zephyr: integration PR #1 (work/dm9051-fixes, the
generic DM9051 fixes) and PR #2 (work/ch390-support, the CH390 support). One
section per upstream commit follows, carrying its rationale. The CH390
devicetree binding (fork commit d8e2dc8313ea) is not part of this diff: it is
a new file, so it lives in patches/zephyr-new/dts/bindings/ethernet/ instead.
When these land upstream, drop the matching sections and regenerate.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: service ISR while the INT line is asserted (fork 939673f2098b) ==

The INT pin is configured edge triggered, while the controller drives
it as a level from the ISR bits enabled in IMR. The RX thread reads
ISR and writes back the bits it read. An event that sets another ISR
bit between the read and the write-back is not cleared, so the line
stays asserted, no further edge arrives and the thread waits on its
semaphore forever. Reception and TX completion both stop. The ISR
accesses are not under spi_lock, so a transmit in progress on the bus
can hold the write-back back for a whole frame transfer and widen that
window. A failed ISR read or write-back leaves the line asserted in the
same way.

After servicing ISR, service it again without waiting while the INT
line is still asserted and the last read showed an interrupt source
enabled in IMR, as the WIZnet drivers do. Bit 7 of ISR is not an
interrupt source and can read as 1, so it is masked out of that test.
Otherwise wait for an edge, but for at most one second, and on a
timeout service ISR if the line is asserted. That recovers a failed
ISR access and any other lost edge, and with nothing pending it costs
at most two GPIO reads per second. Espressif's driver for the WCH
CH390, which shares this register map, bounds its wait and checks the
INT level the same way:
https://github.com/espressif/esp-eth-drivers/blob/6c6e77f97ed4c56142140ee14e9ef7f3a4451b7b/ch390/src/esp_eth_mac_ch390.c#L892-L896

Tested on an Elecrow ThinkNode M7 (ESP32-S3 with a WCH CH390, driven by
this driver with local changes for that part). Without this change
reception stopped for good within seconds under TCP traffic or a ping
flood, and once 8 s after boot with no load: the INT line was held
with PR and ROS, and in some runs PT, pending in ISR, IMR was 0xa3,
and the RX thread was waiting on its semaphore. With the immediate
re-service, 13 minutes of TCP traffic, pings and floods took 94776
wakeups, 3719 of which re-serviced ISR without waiting for an edge,
and reception never stalled. Not tested on a DM9051.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: service ISR when the INT line read fails (fork 8d90d172d38f) ==

On the RX thread's wait timeout, a failed gpio_pin_get_dt() read was
treated as an inactive line, so ISR was not serviced and the thread
waited again. Repeated read failures could then defer servicing
indefinitely. Only a read of zero now counts as inactive, so a read
error services ISR, at most once per wait period.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: set the INT polarity at init (fork 6698a1a76d36) ==

The RX thread now checks the INT line level when its bounded wait
times out, and it starts before the interface is started. INTCR keeps
its reset polarity until hw_start() programs it from the GPIO flags, so
on a board with an active low INT pin the idle line read as asserted
and ISR was serviced once a second until the interface was started.
Program the polarity in init as well, right after the chip ID check.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: drop RX frames too large for the buffer (fork 540ca4fa0c39) ==

eth_dm9051_recv_pkt() used the size of its RX buffer as the limit for
a trustworthy frame length. Without CONFIG_NET_VLAN that buffer is
1518 bytes, but with RCR.DIS_LONG set the controller still stores
somewhat longer frames, such as a full size frame carrying an 802.1Q
tag. A valid frame of 1519 bytes or more was therefore treated like a
corrupt header and the controller was restarted.

Restart only for a length above the 1536 byte limit that the Linux
driver also uses, DM9051_PKT_MAX, or below the minimum frame size:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/d24e8ac715de2e16a53c144005b1863660a5fbea/drivers/net/ethernet/davicom/dm9051.h#L150
A frame within that limit but larger than the buffer is read past
and dropped, as the allocation failure path already does.

Observed on an Elecrow ThinkNode M7 (ESP32-S3 with a WCH CH390, driven
by this driver with local changes for that part), with the RX read
pointer traced. A 1521 byte multicast frame with status 0x40 restarted
the controller, which on the CH390 took the link down for 1.6 s and
caused a new DHCP lease. The read pointer had
advanced by exactly the previous frame plus its header, so the length
was genuine. Not tested on a DM9051.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: keep draining RX after an oversized frame (fork 0b96f2634ac7) ==

Dropping a frame larger than the RX buffer ended the RX drain. ISR has
already been written back at that point, so the frames queued behind
the dropped one stayed in RX SRAM until an unrelated frame raised a
new receive interrupt; on a quiet link that can take arbitrarily long.

The read pointer is correct after the drop, so continue the drain
instead. eth_dm9051_recv_pkt() now returns an error code and the packet
through an output argument, and -EMSGSIZE marks a dropped frame, which
is counted as an RX error. The other failure paths still end the
drain.

Built for native_sim and native_sim/native/64 with the build_all
ethernet SPI test.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: restart the controller when an RX read fails (fork e1af066438a4) ==

Each MRCMD read advances the RX SRAM read pointer. When the header,
discard or data read of a frame failed, eth_dm9051_recv_pkt() only
returned the error, and the pointer was no longer known to be at a
frame boundary. The next drain then parsed frame data as a header, or,
where bit 0 of the byte at the pointer was clear, found no frame and
left reception stalled. That includes the discard of an oversized
frame, which this series added in place of a restart.

Restart the controller after a failed MRCMD read, as the error path for
an untrusted header already does, so that reception resumes at a frame
boundary. The restart moves to a shared label. The MRCMDX flag read
does not move the pointer, and its failure still only ends the drain.
A restart renegotiates the link. A failed transfer that moved no bytes
now pays that cost too, since the SPI API does not report how many
bytes were clocked.

Built for native_sim and native_sim/native/64 with the build_all
ethernet SPI test, and for esp32s3_devkitc/esp32s3/procpu with
warnings as errors. The changed paths need a failed SPI transfer and
were not exercised on hardware.

== drivers: ethernet: dm9051: add WCH CH390 support (fork 3294067c9f16) ==

The WCH CH390 has the register map of the DM9051 but reports product ID
0x9151, so eth_dm9051_check_id() rejects it. Carry the expected product
ID in the instance config and instantiate the driver for the wch,ch390
compatible as well, the same way eth_adin2111.c serves the ADIN2111 and
the ADIN1110. Both product ID log messages now print the ID converted
from little endian.

The CH390 also behaves differently in three places, each handled for
the CH390 only so the DM9051 path is unchanged. Espressif's CH390
driver takes the same approach to each, though the order of its setup
writes differs.

- Its software reset powers the PHY down again. MDIO keeps answering
  but the link never comes up. Power the PHY on again after the reset,
  once the INT polarity is restored, and wait 10 ms before writing the
  remaining registers:
  https://github.com/espressif/esp-eth-drivers/blob/6c6e77f97ed4c56142140ee14e9ef7f3a4451b7b/ch390/src/esp_eth_mac_ch390.c#L386-L389

- It leaves NSR TX1END and TX2END clear after reset, so the pre-transmit
  NSR poll never passes and no frame is ever sent. Wait for TCR.TXREQ of
  the previous frame to clear instead. A full frame takes about 1.2 ms
  at 10 Mbit/s, so the wait busy polls against a 2 ms cycle counter
  deadline with WAIT_FOR() rather than sleeping in system ticks:
  https://github.com/espressif/esp-eth-drivers/blob/6c6e77f97ed4c56142140ee14e9ef7f3a4451b7b/ch390/src/esp_eth_mac_ch390.c#L763-L773

- A FIFO overflow sets an RX status error, and restarting the
  controller for it powers the PHY down, so every overflow cost a link
  outage and a new DHCP lease. Skip such a frame by its length instead
  and keep draining. The controller is still restarted when the length
  is out of range, when the header's ready byte is not 0x01, or when
  the skip fails. eth_dm9051_recv_pkt() reports a skipped frame as
  -EBADMSG, and the drain continues after it as it does after a dropped
  oversized frame. pkt is initialized in eth_dm9051_rx() because GCC
  cannot prove it set once two codes continue the drain
  (-Werror=maybe-uninitialized on native_sim):
  https://github.com/espressif/esp-eth-drivers/blob/6c6e77f97ed4c56142140ee14e9ef7f3a4451b7b/ch390/src/esp_eth_mac_ch390.c#L812-L815

Tested on an Elecrow ThinkNode M7 (ESP32-S3 with a CH390), adding one
difference at a time:
- From a power-on reset, without the PHY power-on there was no link:
  BMSR 0x7849, LPA 0x0001, and GPR read 0x05 right after the reset
  although PHY_ON had been written before it. With it, the link came
  up at 100 Mbps full duplex about 2.1 s after boot.
- From a power-on reset, with the NSR poll the link was up but every
  poll timed out with NSR=0x40, no TX request was written and DHCP
  never completed. With the TCR.TXREQ wait, DHCP completed about 5 s
  after boot, and the longest single wait measured under a ping flood
  was 213 us.
- With the restart, 30 s of 1400-byte pings every 2 ms produced RX
  status 0x01, the link was down for 1.9 s and DHCP ran again. With the
  skip, 13 minutes of TCP traffic with two such floods dropped one frame
  with status 0x41, with no restart and no link event, and 600 of 600
  pings were answered.

